    {
      "source": "SlowMist",
      "text": "The attacker created a malicious contract masquerading as an ERC20 token, and the \"transfer\" function re-invokes the payment channel contract repeatedly, each time exhausting some ETH."
    },
    {
      "source": "RevertIQ",
      "text": "SpankChain's payment channel contract on Ethereum Mainnet was exploited on 7 October 2018 via a reentrancy vulnerability. The attacker used a malicious token contract to repeatedly withdraw funds, draining 165 ETH (~$38,000 at the time). All stolen funds, including ETH and BOOTY tokens, were later recovered after the attacker provided the private key to SpankChain. The LedgerChannel contract violated the Checks-Effects-Interactions pattern by calling an external token's transfer function before deleting channel state within the LCOpenTimeout() function. This allowed a malicious token contract to re-enter LCOpenTimeout and repeatedly withdraw funds from…"
    }
  ],
